<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ariane (rocket family)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1979">1979</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ariane is a series of European civilian launch vehicles developed for the European Space Agency, first flown in 1979. All Ariane rockets lift off from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guiana-space-centre">Guiana Space Centre</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kourou">Kourou</a> in French Guiana, close to the equator. Ariane 4 and Ariane 5 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> a leading provider of commercial satellite launches, and Ariane 6 first flew in 2024.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ariane-rocket-family.jpg" alt="Ariane (rocket family)" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: uploaded by Rama, from NASA photo. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ariane4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> first proposed the Ariane project, and it was officially agreed at the end of 1973 after discussions between France, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the UK. The name is the French spelling of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariadne">Ariadne</a>. The project was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>'s second attempt at its own launcher, after the unsuccessful Europa project, and was code-named L3S, the French abbreviation for third-generation substitution launcher. The European Space Agency charged Aérospatiale with developing all Ariane launchers and their test facilities, while Arianespace handled production, operations and marketing after its creation in 1980. Following the merger of 2000 that founded Airbus, its space branch and then ArianeGroup took over the duties of Aérospatiale.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ariane 1 was a three-stage launcher derived from ballistic missile technology: the first two stages used hypergolic propellants and the third burned liquid hydrogen and liquid oxygen. Ariane 2 to 4 were enhancements with improved engines, stretched tanks and greater payloads, and the largest could launch two satellites mounted in the SPELDA adapter. Strap-on boosters, solid or liquid, are shown by suffixes, so an Ariane 42P has two solid boosters. Ariane 5 was a nearly complete redesign in which a single core stage with one Vulcain engine replaced the two hypergolic lower stages, with two solid-fuel boosters at the sides that can be recovered for examination but are not reused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Ariane rockets are launched by Arianespace from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guiana-space-centre">Guiana Space Centre</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kourou">Kourou</a> in French Guiana. By January 2006, 169 flights had boosted 290 satellites. In 1996 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s Cerise satellite, orbited by an Ariane 4 in 1995, struck a discarded Ariane stage, the first verified collision with catalogued space debris. On 4 May 2007 an Ariane 5-ECA set a commercial payload record by lifting two satellites with a combined mass of 9.4 tonnes. The 200th Ariane launch, on 16 February 2011, carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler">Johannes Kepler</a> supply craft to the International Space Station, and on 25 December 2021 an Ariane lifted the James Webb Space Telescope towards the Lagrange point L2. Ariane 5 flew its final mission on 5 July 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ariane (rocket family)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Guiana Space Centre opened at Kourou in 1968 and now launches European <b>Ariane</b> rockets.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-guiana">French Guiana</a></li></ul></section>
